```

- Set `baseUrl` to the URL of your application (e.g., `http://localhost:3000`).
- Disable Chrome Web Security since Cypress will run in a Docker container.
- Disable video recording since Docker does not support video recording.
- Set up the `mocha-junit-reporter` for test reporting.
- Set `testFileNamePattern` to use the test name as the file name.
- Set a longer `testTimeout` to account for potential delays during Docker setup.
- Configure Cypress Docker support by specifying the image (`node:14.17.0`), network (`cypress`), and volumes (`/app` for your project files and `/var/run/docker.sock` for Docker API access).

5. Create a new Cypress plugin file, `cypress/plugins/index.js`, to capture screenshots on test failure:

```javascript
const { addTask } = require("@cypress/base");

module.exports = (on) => {
  on("task", {
    captureScreenshot: () => {
      Cypress.runnerContext().then((context) => {
        const screenshotPath = `${context.specFile.relative}/${context.test.title}.png`;
        return Cypress.util.writeFile(
          Cypress.config("screenshotsDir"),
          screenshotPath,
          context.screenshots.base64[0],
          "base64"
        );
      });
    },
  });

  addTask({
    title: "capture-screenshots-on-failure",
    description: "Capture screenshots on test failure",
    task: async ({ subject }) => {
      if (subject.result.status !== "failed") {
        return;
      }

      await Cypress.task("captureScreenshot");
    },
    priority: 100,
  });
};
```

This plugin adds a new Cypress task, `captureScreenshot`, that saves a screenshot to the `screenshotsDir` when a test fails. The task is added to Cypress's task queue using `addTask` and has a high priority to ensure screenshots are captured promptly.

